    "body": "**TLDR:** A generator weight the trainer didn't publish now fails the first weight pull, naming the keys, instead of silently running at its init value. No recipe hits this today; it guards against the trainer and generator model configs diverging.\n\n**Problem**\n1. The generator pulls weights with `strict=False`: TorchStore fetches only the keys that are both published and in the generator's pinned CPU dict.\n2. A generator key the trainer didn't publish keeps its old value, with no error.\n3. `load_state_dict(strict=True)` can't see it, because the pinned dict comes from the generator's own model.\n4. In RL there is no HF load on the generator, so the old value is the init: random weights, or zeros for an `expert_bias_E` buffer, so routing can differ from the trainer's.\n5. Nothing hits this today: 0 unpublished keys across 32 RL recipes. But the trainer and generator model configs are separate objects, and the controller compares only their class, so a per-role config change can open the gap.\n\n**Solution**\n1. `prefetch_model_state_dict` compares the pinned dict's keys with the keys the pull returned, and raises `RuntimeError` naming the first 5.\n2. vLLM's attention scales (`.vllm_attn.` keys) exist only in the generator, so they are exempt.\n3. Keys the trainer publishes but the generator doesn't hold (e.g. a vision encoder) aren't fetched, so they aren't checked.\n4. Unchanged: the weights, the pinned dict, `load_state_dict`.\n\n**Results**\n\n```text\ntrainer vs generator keys, 32 RL recipes (meta device, CPU):   unpublished keys 0\nsame probe, 9 recipes in dapo_math, search_r1, verifiers_dapo_math\n  and verifiers_terminal_bench:                               unpublished keys 0\n  (not built: the DeepEP search_r1 perf recipe, which needs DeepEP v2, and the Muse Spark GB300\n  recipe, which takes arguments; the Terminal-Bench recipes were built with Verifiers stubbed)\ncheck cost per pull (1920 keys, CPU):                          0.03 ms\n\n2x H100, trainer 1 GPU + generator 1 GPU, 2 train steps:\n  Kimi K3 debug (MoE, 16 expert_bias_E buffers)   3 pulls, no error\n  Qwen3-0.6B (tied lm_head.weight)                3 pulls, no error\n  trainer drops layers.1.feed_forward.expert_bias_E:\n    RuntimeError: The trainer did not publish 1 of the generator's weights (first 5: ['layers.1.feed_forward.expert_bias_E']). Check that the trainer and generator model configs match.\n  trainer drops lm_head.weight (tied):\n    RuntimeError: The trainer did not publish 1 of the generator's weights (first 5: ['lm_head.weight']). ...\n```\n\n<details><summary>Key probe (per recipe)</summary>\n\n```python\nconfig = recipe()\nwith set_current_vllm_config(VllmConfig()), torch.device(\"meta\"):\n    trainer = config.trainer.model.build()\n    generator = _replace_vllm_layer_configs(config.generator.model).build()\nrequired = {key for key in generator.state_dict() if \".vllm_attn.\" not in key}\nassert not required - set(trainer.state_dict())\n```\n\nRun over every `rl_grpo*` recipe in `torchtitan_recipes/rl/alphabet_sort.py` and `torchtitan_recipes/tests/rl/alphabet_sort.py`: 32 of 35 build (3 need `dist_moe`, DeepEP or a newer torchao).\n</details>\n\nYou may ask:\n- Why not check parameters only? That misses persistent buffers (16 `expert_bias_E` in the Kimi K3 debug model) and the tied `lm_head.weight`.\n- Why check on every pull? It costs microseconds, and checking once would need a flag.\n- Why not `strict=True` in TorchStore? The routing client doesn't support it: each rank reads only the keys it registered.\n\n**Test plan**\n- `pytest tests/rl/unit_tests/cpu/test_generator.py::test_prefetch_model_state_dict_raises_when_a_weight_is_not_published` (fails on main).\n- `pytest tests/rl/unit_tests/cpu/test_generator.py tests/rl/unit_tests/cpu/test_engine_loop.py`: 87 passed, 1 skipped.\n- Smoke: local recipe overrides of `rl_grpo_kimi_k3_debug_varlen` and `rl_grpo_qwen3_0_6b_varlen` (trainer 1 GPU + generator 1 GPU, 2 steps), plus the two negative controls above (a local edit dropping a key from the trainer's staging dict).\n\n",
